# Local env files must never reach the deployment bundle.
#
# Two reasons:
# 1. Security: .env holds real credentials, which have no business being in a
#    deployment artifact.
# 2. Correctness: the Next.js runtime loads .env. Platform-injected variables
#    take precedence, so most variables look "normal" - but a variable that
#    exists ONLY in .env and is not set on the platform still takes effect.
#    That is how RESEND_BASE_URL leaked into production: it pointed at
#    http://localhost:4010, every send failed, and the SDK swallowed the
#    underlying exception into a vague "Unable to fetch data" that took a long
#    time to trace.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.local.example

# Local build output
node_modules
.next
lib/generated

# Tests and local-only scripts; not needed in production
tests
scripts/mock-resend.mjs
scripts/demo.mjs
